sor-projection-initiator-alias-mismatch</code> |\n12320|| logs | initiator | cursor-projection | resolved session UUID | session_id | equals resolved session_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-initiator-session-mismatch</code> |\n12321|| logs | initiator | cursor-projection | resolved record digest | session_record_id | equals resolved session_record_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-initiator-record-mismatch</code> |\n12322|| logs | initiator | cursor-projection | resolved source host | source_host_id | equals resolved source_host_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-initiator-source-mismatch</code> |\n12323|| logs | receiver | initial-projection | resolved session UUID | session_id | equals resolved session_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-receiver-session-mismatch</code> |\n12324|| logs | receiver | initial-projection | resolved record digest | session_record_id | equals resolved session_record_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-receiver-record-mismatch</code> |\n12325|| logs | receiver | initial-projection | route logs | action | equals logs | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-receiver-action-mismatch</code> |\n12326|| logs | receiver | initial-projection | explicit --peer host | destination_host_id | equals peer_host per-endpoint | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-receiver-destination-mismatch</code> |\n12327|| logs | receiver | cursor-projection | resolved session UUID | session_id | equals resolved session_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-receiver-session-mismatch</code> |\n12328|| logs | receiver | cursor-projection | resolved record digest | session_record_id | equals resolved session_record_id | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-receiver-record-mismatch</code> |\n12329|| logs | receiver | cursor-projection | route logs with cursor | action | equals logs | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-receiver-action-mismatch</code> |\n12330|| logs | receiver | cursor-projection | explicit --peer host with cursor | destination_host_id | equals peer_host per-endpoint | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-receiver-destination-mismatch</code> |\n12331|| logs | both | initial-projection | route logs | action | both equal logs | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-both-action-mismatch</code> |\n12332|| logs | both | initial-projection | explicit --peer host | destination_host_id | both equal peer_host | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-projection-both-destination-mismatch</code> |\n12333|| logs | both | cursor-projection | route logs with cursor | action | both equal logs | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-both-action-mismatch</code> |\n12334|| logs | both | cursor-projection | explicit --peer host with cursor | destination_host_id | both equal peer_host | <code>SEL-CASE-logs-cursor-projection-both-destination-mismatch</code> |\n12335|\n12336|Other managed-session remote action routes use Section 11's existing typed\n12337|session_id and LeaseExpectation/transaction IDs; they MUST carry the selected\n12338|UUID and existing expected authority, never convert either into a CLI NAME.\n12339|Their closed RPC envelopes and bound error versions are unchanged. Selector\n12340|failures are emitted locally as CLI5/Error1.4, not inserted into an older RPC.\n12341|\n12342|#### 14.7.3 Authoritative summaries and closed failure binding\n12343|\n12344|Selector-capable CLI defaults to CLI Result 5.0.0 and Structured Error 1.4.0.\n12345|Explicit <code>--result-version VERSION</code> selects a supported exact CLI\n12346|Result version before parsing command operands; unsupported versions fail locally\n12347|with incompatible_schema. Version 5 retains the exact v4 success envelope,\n12348|command/body registry and SessionSummary shape. It changes selector admission and\n12349|requires authoritative projection below. A canceled chooser emits the selected\n12350|record's existing NAME in the unchanged cancel body, not the qualified argument.\n12351|There is no successful public incomplete-session variant and no nullable owner,\n12352|lease epoch zero, synthetic lease ID, or added SessionState.\n12353|\n12354|Every SessionSummary owner_host_id, lease_epoch and lease_id comes from the\n12355|validated winning Lease Record, including the initial epoch-1 create lease.\n12356|owner_host_name comes from validated host metadata for that holder; missing host\n12357|metadata is a refusal, never the source alias. local_role compares that holder\n12358|with the local host UUID. Before the first checkpoint, both newest-checkpoint\n12359|members are null; creating is valid only with an authoritative initial lease\n12360|and the existing eligible-bootstrap facts. A Session Record alone proves\n12361|identity/provenance, not creation of an owner or a creating lifecycle. Liveness\n12362|and capabilities require the existing observation/evidence contracts; source\n12363|reachability, a cached row or an absent observation cannot mint process_present,\n12364|readiness, capability, or current ownership. An unknown required observation is\n12365|selector_observation_unavailable. Read-only inspection never creates a lease.\n12366|\n12367|A complete read proving a record without any valid lease returns\n12368|selector_bootstrap_incomplete; a failed read follows the local/remote read classification above,\n12369|and corrupt/inconsistent authority returns integrity_failure/lease_conflict as\n12370|applicable. List refuses the whole success document if an encountered record\n12371|cannot be represented truthfully; it MUST NOT silently omit that record, invent\n12372|summary fields, or use partial/unreachable_peer_ids to conceal a record-only\n12373|bootstrap. Existing legitimate peer-partial list semantics otherwise remain.\n12374|\n12375|Structured Error 1.4.0 keeps the exact 1.3 top-level shape and all earlier\n12376|code-to-exit mappings and adds exactly:\n12377|\n12378|| Exit | Code | Required meaning |\n12379|| ---: | --- | --- |\n12380|| 4 | <code>selector_source_not_found</code> | Complete valid config has no requested source |\n12381|| 8 | <code>selector_source_read_failed</code> | Remote source transport/I/O read failed; no fallback; local I/O uses local_precondition_failed and partial/malformed evidence uses integrity_failure |\n12382|| 12 | <code>selector_bootstrap_incomplete</code> | Complete authority read proves record-only creation; inspect/recover original bootstrap |\n12383|| 3 | <code>selector_observation_unavailable</code> | Required summary observation or host metadata cannot be established |\n12384|| 16 | <code>selector_plan_stale</code> | Bound selection facts changed; fresh plan/confirmation required |\n12385|\n12386|These five errors have retryable=false; known session/operation IDs are included.\n12387|Redacted details may explain phase/source without secrets or inventing facts.\n12388|Only CLI Result 5 binds Error 1.4; selector errors MUST NOT be emitted inside\n12389|legacy RPC/Provider/Directory envelopes. CLI Result 1/2/3/4 retain exact historical\n12390|syntax, body and Error 1.0/1.1/1.2/1.3 bindings respectively. An explicitly selected\n12391|legacy result version refuses id:/qualified selector syntax with its bound\n12392|invalid_arguments, never silently treats it as a name. Unrepresentable summaries\n12393|on legacy surfaces refuse with that surface's existing local_precondition_failed\n12394|(exit 3); incompatible_schema covers unsupported representation versions.\n12395|No lower-version successful projection may fabricate missing authority. Record,\n12396|lease, event and error objects already published are never rewritten/re-digested.\n12397|\n12398|#### 14.7.4 Interrupted record-only creation\n12399|\n12400|The crash gap inside Section 13.1 step 2 (Session Record durable, initial Lease\n12401|Record not yet durable) is recovery work, before creating. Recovery runs only on\n12402|the original creating host under a serialized machine-local BootstrapIntent\n12403|and its original operation IDs. Selector contract 1.0.0 defines this closed\n12404|local representation: selector_version (exact 1.0.0), session_record_id (digest),\n12405|workspace_group_record_id (digest), creating_host_id (UUIDv7),\n12406|bootstrap_operation_id (UUIDv7), first_checkpoint_operation_id (UUIDv7), and\n12407|initial_lease (the complete allocated Lease Record 1.0.0 described by the\n12408|closed bootstrap representation table below).\n12409|New creation MUST atomically persist and fsync this intent and its parent\n12410|directory before publishing the Session Record. Retain it through durable\n12411|session.created publication and reconcile against immutable authority on retry.\n12412|It is never replicated, never a lease grant, and never accepted from caller\n12413|bytes as recovery authority. Its encoding is canonical JSON under selector\n12414|contract 1.0.0, not an extension of a historical journal. Legacy record-only\n12415|creation without this original intent remains refused; no migration invents it. It requires a complete valid authority read proving no lease,\n12416|no terminal/provider effect and no conflicting creation; an unavailable read\n12417|cannot establish any of those absences. It reuses the already allocated/persisted\n12418|initial lease and operation IDs, validates the Session Record/Workspace Group,\n12419|and durably publishes the initial epoch-1 create lease and session.created in the\n12420|original order before any terminal/provider action. The same intent ordering\n12421|applies to task-board creation in Section 13.2 step 1. If the original durable\n12422|bootstrap inputs are missing, ambiguous or inconsistent, remain refused with\n12423|selector_bootstrap_incomplete for manual diagnosis; do not allocate replacement\n12424|IDs, infer the creator is owner, recover from another peer, or launch a second\n12425|process. When the lease already exists, reconcile it and the original intent;\n12426|never issue a second initial lease. A lease/event read failure is not the\n12427|record-only case. Normal Section 13.1 bootstrap retry begins only after these\n12428|facts are established. No new recovery command, replicated journal schema, or\n12429|cross-host create authority is introduced.\n12430|\n12431|The exact BootstrapIntent members are the seven members above, all required;\n12432|unknown members are refused. Its embedded Lease Record retains every required\n12433|Section 5.3 member, including nullable members, canonical self digest,\n12434|UUIDv4 fencing token, timestamp and reverse-DNS extensions. The following\n12435|initial-lease values and cross-record bindings are normative, and are consumed\n12436|by the publication evaluator in addition to structural validation:\n12437|\n12438|| Bootstrap constraint | Required value |\n12439|| --- | --- |\n12440|| epoch | 1 |\n12441|| reason | create |\n12442|| predecessor_lease_id | null |\n12443|| checkpoint_id | null |\n12444|| schema | urn:ax:schema:lease |\n12445|| schema_version | 1.0.0 |\n12446|\n12447|Intent session_record_id and workspace_group_record_id must equal the canonical\n12448|record digests of the supplied Session/Workspace Group. Session subject_id equals\n12449|session_id; Workspace Group subject_id equals workspace_group_id; the Session's\n12450|workspace_group_id equals that group's ID and its launch cwd names a member.\n12451|Lease session_id and subject_id equal the Session ID. Intent creating_host_id,\n12452|Session created_by_host_id, lease holder_host_id, issued_by_host_id and\n12453|created_by_host_id equal the original creating host and the recovering local\n12454|host. An existing reused Workspace Group need not have the same creator.\n12455|Both operation IDs are distinct UUIDv7 and equal the IDs in the original durable\n12456|creation inputs; initial_lease is byte-identical after canonicalization to the\n12457|originally allocated lease. Recovery compares the whole original intent, not\n12458|just its digest, against trusted machine-local original inputs. Rehashed caller\n12459|records, fresh operation IDs or a fresh valid lease cannot replace them.\n12460|Absent or malformed original intent, invalid embedded lease, or inconsistent\n12461|bindings refuse selector_bootstrap_incomplete before publication. A failed read\n12462|of those inputs retains its read-failure class, not absence. Existing-lease\n12463|reconciliation first validates the same intent and must not republish a lease.\n12464|The original-input provenance, serialization, effect absence and complete\n12465|observation checks remain runtime prerequisites; concrete synthetic record\n12466|validation does not prove those runtime facts.\n12467|\n12468|#### 14.7.5 Publication conformance and assurance bounds\n12469|\n12470|The following closed machine-readable policy is normative for the selector\n12471|reference evaluator in the publication gate. The policy is the executable normative admission/refusal representation for\n12472|the eight listed source-conformance families. The guard expression grammar is\n12473|closed: get reads a required context field; eq/ne compare JSON values; gt\n12474|compares counts; all/any combine booleans. Guard lists evaluate in order and\n12475|refuse with the first matching error. Missing fields, unknown operators/stages,\n12476|or malformed policy are publication failures. Prose explains these predicates\n12477|and additionally specifies runtime authority/durability obligations outside the\n12478|source evaluator. Prose and policy must agree; neither\n12479|synthetic vectors nor source fingerprints prove a running AX implementation.\n12480|The fixture families below are the independently enumerated publication scope.\n12481|\n12482|| ID | Publication obligation |\n12483|| --- | --- |\n12484|| SEL-GRAMMAR | First-at grammar, exact aliases, explicit durable UUIDs and input bounds |\n12485|| SEL-PRECEDENCE | Bare tier order, collisions and tombstones |\n12486|| SEL-SOURCE | Missing/read-failed/empty source and no explicit-source fallback |\n12487|| SEL-PLAN | Resolve once and all bound facts revalidated before effects |\n12488|| SEL-AUTHORITY | Source is neither owner nor destination |\n12489|| SEL-SUMMARY | Initial lease/observation projection and truthful refusal |\n12490|| SEL-BOOTSTRAP | Interrupted record-only recovery with original durable inputs |\n12491|| SEL-VERSION | Exact versions and closed historical behavior |\n12492|\n12493|\n12494|The exact source-only case inventory is closed; dropping a case is a publication\n12495|failure, not a reduction of the denominator.\n12496|\n12497|Publication composition derives its action/boundary and SelectionPlan member\n12498|inventory from Section 14.7.2, including both CLI5 remote routes. Each endpoint\n12499|inherits the action boundaries except the opposite transport endpoint:\n12500|remote-admission belongs to the receiver, remote-dispatch to the initiator.\n12501|Session-filtered logs require separate initial and cursor-continuation witnesses.\n12502|Every resulting row requires an unchanged positive, revoked and forged-plan\n12503|negatives, failed/partial/malformed reads in each local/remote domain, and one\n12504|changed and one missing current-fact witness for every bound SelectionPlan member.\n12505|The opposite endpoint remains valid. These synthetic vectors must execute the\n12506|composed publication evaluator, not only generic revalidation. Caller and generic\n12507|checks must independently resist scope narrowing. Recovery is measured for the\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
